   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to prohibiting\nsymbols of hate on state owned property at the state fair; and to amend a\nchapter of the laws of 2020, amending the public buildings law and the\nagriculture and markets law relating to prohibiting the state of New York from\nselling or displaying symbols of hate, as proposed in legislative bills\nnumbers S.8298-B and A.10729-A, in relation to making the provisions of such\nact severable",
    "basePrintNo": "S883",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S883-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §16, Ag & Mkts L; amd §3, Chap of 2020 (as proposed in S.8298-B & A.10729-A)",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S883\n\nSPONSOR: BIAGGI\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to prohib-\niting symbols of hate on state owned property at the state fair; and to\namend a chapter of the laws of 2020, amending the public buildings law\nand the agriculture and markets law relating to prohibiting the state of\nNew York from selling  or displaying symbols of hate, as proposed in\nlegislative bills numbers S.8298-B and A.10729-A, in relation to making\nthe provisions of such act severable\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis is a chapter amendment that makes changes to provisions of L.2020,\nC.343.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis legislation amends the underlying chapter by removing the prohibi-\ntion against symbols of hate being sold at the State Fair, and instead\nwould prohibit any individual from attaching or affixing a symbol of\nhate, or any other similar image, to state property at the State Fair.\nAdditionally, this proposal also removes the language prohibiting\nsymbols of hate or any other similar images from being sold by vendors\nat other publicly-funded fairs in New York State, like county fairs.\nLastly, this proposal would amend the chapter by adding a severability\nclause.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPursuant to approval memo 47 of 2020, the Executive expressed constitu-\ntional concerns over a content-based legislative ban on symbols of hate.\nHowever, the Executive does support, the intent behind the original\nproposal, of creating a safe and family-friendly atmosphere at the State\nFair. To honor the original intent of the legislation within the strict\nconstitutional limits on the legislature's ability to enact content-\nbased prohibitions, this proposal prohibits any individual from attach-\ning or affixing a symbol of hate, or any other similar image, to state\nproperty at the State Fair.\n\nThe Executive also expressed enforceability concerns on extending this\nproposal to all publicly-funded fairs in the state, noting that the\nState Fair is uniquely under the State's directive, unlike the many\nother public fairs across the state. Lastly, this proposal amends the\nunderlying chapter with the introduction of a severability clause, at\nthe Executive's request.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nChapter amendment to Chapter 343 of the Laws of 2020.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the same date and in the same manner as a\nchapter of the laws of 2020, amending the public buildings law and the\nagriculture and markets law relating to prohibiting the state of New\nYork from selling or displaying symbols of hate, as proposed in legisla-\ntive bills number S.8298-B and A.10729-A, takes effect.\n",
